{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Bound the memory sum on the rebar service host\n\nThe $0 half of epic `couped-subzero-wolf` — no hardware is resized; this is what makes any\ninstance size hold. Four enforcement points, each sized from measurement.\n\n## Measured basis (CloudWatch `rebar/host`, i-00880b2c7f13527c5)\n\n`container_memory_rss_bytes`, 48h Average / Maximum over 2026-09-03T17:00Z - 2026-09-05:\n\n| service | avg | max |\n|---|---|---|\n| gerrit | 0.92 GB | 1.19 GB |\n| mcp | 1.66 GB | **4.64 GB** |\n| review-bot | 1.07 GB | **2.43 GB** |\n| opcert | 0.041 GB | 0.048 GB |\n| **sum of maxima** | | **8.31 GB** |\n\nRAM is 7.8 GiB = 8.37 GB: **the sum of observed peaks is 99% of physical memory.**\n`mem_available_percent` hourly minima on the outage day were 11-13% for seven hours.\n\nGate transients live INSIDE `mcp` and `review-bot` (both execute gates) — hence maxima 4.3x\nand 15x their quiet baselines. Today `max_concurrent_gates` resolves to the code default 4\n(no `[snapshot]` in `rebar.toml`), no service declares `mem_limit`, and `gate_admission.py`\nadmits on a count alone.\n\n## The probe cannot be trusted to detect this\n\nSame probe runs, 2026-09-05 12:00-16:00 PDT: `mcp_healthy` (head of `observability.sh`)\npublished 7-12 samples/hour throughout while `mem_available_percent` (tail) published **zero\ndatapoints for five consecutive hours**. Next three hours on a healthy idle box: `mcp_healthy`\n29/36 buckets (81%), `mem_available_percent` 5/36 (14%).\n\nThe probe did not die — it is SIGTERM-ed on its 240s budget before the memory read (bug\n`ignitable-fuchsia-kawala`; fix in change 2684). The memory signal is absent 73-86% of the\ntime and **its absence is anti-correlated with the condition it measures**, so a `breaching`\nalarm on it is a missing-data pager whose noise invites the very window-widening epic 0480\nexists to stop. Discriminator this buys: head present + memory absent = truncated; both\nabsent = probe dead. The dead-man is built ON that.\n\n## Changes\n\n**1. `rebar.toml`** — `[snapshot] max_concurrent_gates = 2`. Removes 2 x 748 MB (measured\n`GATE_PEAK_RSS`) from the permitted worst case: 8.31 -> 6.83 GB (82% of RAM), moving the\navailable floor from ~11% to ~18%. Two not one, because change 3 refuses the second slot when\nmemory is already low, so the count need not carry the bound alone.\n\n**2. `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml` + `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`** — `mem_limit` on\nall four services, AND `--memory` on the mcp blue-green `docker run`.\n\nA compose limit alone would NOT reach mcp, the service driving this plan: `mcp_run_new()` is\na bare `docker run -d` whose own comment says \"compose never sees\" it. The other three go\nthrough `docker compose up`. The existing parity oracle\n`test_autodeploy_mcp_bluegreen.py::test_docker_run_matches_compose_mcp_service` is extended to\npin `--memory` to compose's `mem_limit` so they cannot drift.\n\n- `gerrit` **3072m** — reservation-driven: `gerrit.config` `heapLimit 2g` + `packedGitLimit\n  1g` = 3 GiB. Measured max 1.19 GB, so 2.6x headroom over observation.\n- `mcp` **2048m** — quiet 0.97 GB + one gate slot 0.75 GB = 1.72 GB, 17% headroom. The\n  4.64 GB observed peak is 2.3x this limit, so that excursion now kills mcp loudly instead of\n  letting the kernel choose gerrit.\n- `review-bot` **1280m** — quiet 0.16 GB + one gate 0.75 GB = 0.91 GB, 41% headroom.\n- `opcert` **256m** — 5x its observed 0.048 GB, flat across 48h.\n- Sum 6656 MiB = 6.5 GiB, leaving **1.3 GiB (17%)** for OS and page cache.\n- Tradeoff: the counter caps gates at 2 TOTAL but per-container limits cannot express a joint\n  bound, so each is sized for ONE gate. Both slots in one container exceeds its limit and\n  OOM-kills it — bounded and attributable, which is the point.\n\n**3. `src/rebar/llm/gate_admission.py` + `errors.py`** — a `MemAvailable` floor read from\n`/proc/meminfo`, refusing with a retryable `LLMError` (never a verdict value) below\n**1024 MiB**: above the whole observed wedge band (13% of 7.8 GiB = 1.01 GB) and above one\ngate's 748 MB peak plus margin.\n\nPrior art, matched deliberately not coincidentally: `autodeploy.sh:606`\n`mcp_mem_available_mb()` already reads the same `/proc/meminfo` `MemAvailable` field against\n`MCP_MEM_MIN_MB` (default **1024**), refuses the blue-green overlap, and publishes\n`mcp_mem_abort` — already alarmed in `monitoring_foxterrier.tf`. This adopts that floor and\nits FAIL-OPEN posture for the gate path, which the deploy path does not cover. Not shared\ncode (shell vs library Python, no import seam); the number and reasoning are, and each cites\nthe other.\n\nReads `/proc/meminfo` directly — not CloudWatch, not psutil: works on a laptop with no CI\nprovider, no new dependency. Absent `/proc/meminfo` (macOS) the check is inapplicable and\nsilent; present-but-unreadable emits the existing disarm marker and ADMITS. The refusal reuses\nthe `GATE_CONGESTED` marker with a `reason` field, not a new token. The floor is a module\nconstant, not a config key unlike `MCP_MEM_MIN_MB`: a new `[snapshot]` key is a new\n`config_key` against the shrink-only ratchet; a shell env var is not censused. The docstring's\nclaim that the counter \"also bounds concurrent gate RSS\" becomes true by construction.\n\n**4. `infra/terraform/monitoring_membound.tf`** — two alarms, both on namespace `rebar/host`\nwith NO dimensions (as the probe publishes them; a one-sided dimension silently unmatches\nforever) and both wiring `alarm_actions` + `ok_actions` to `aws_sns_topic.alerts`.\n\n- Threshold: `mem_available_percent < 15`, `notBreaching` (with the required opt-out marker\n  naming the dead-man as owner of absence), 1 of 6 x 300s. 15 is the only integer separating\n  the 11-13% wedge band from every non-incident hourly minimum in the metric's whole history\n  (lowest 16%). Detection latency is CONTINGENT on change 2684 restoring coverage; the re-tune\n  target (3 of 6, still satisfying invariant I3) is recorded in the file. NOT widened for quiet.\n- Dead-man, on the discriminator: metric math\n  `IF(FILL(mem_available_percent,-1) < 0 AND FILL(mcp_healthy,0) > 0, 1, 0)` — 1 means \"the\n  probe ran and the memory reading is still missing\". Probe wholly dead evaluates to 0 and\n  stays silent, that case belonging to `mcp_serving_path_down`. Window 36 x 300s = 3h, sized\n  from measurement: across the metric's entire history the ONLY >= 3h zero-datapoint span is\n  the incident, and this would have fired ~3h into the 5-hour silence, before the wedge.\n  Metric-math alarms declare namespace in nested `metric_query` blocks, so this one is\n  excluded by construction from the top-level-namespace guards, as `monitoring_eb6e.tf`'s\n  Bedrock alarm already is.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates = 2` in `rebar.toml`, resolving to 2.\n- [ ] AC2 All four compose services declare `mem_limit`, their sum leaving >= 1 GiB free —\n      `tests/unit/test_compose_memory_limits.py`.\n- [ ] AC3 The mcp blue-green `docker run` carries `--memory` equal to compose's `mem_limit`,\n      per the extended parity oracle. Without this the mcp limit is never in force.\n- [ ] AC4 `gate_admission` refuses below the floor with retryable semantics and admits above\n      it; the refusal is never a verdict value. New cases in `test_gate_admission_09da.py`.\n- [ ] AC5 The check ADMITS when `/proc/meminfo` is ABSENT (silently) and, as a separate\n      branch, when PRESENT BUT UNREADABLE (emitting the disarm marker). Named tests for both.\n- [ ] AC6 Both alarms exist with actions wired; `test_alarm_window_tuning.py` and\n      `test_alarm_actions_terraform.py` pass with their invariants unweakened.\n- [ ] AC7 `terraform validate` passes; terraform is NOT applied here. AC8 `make verify` passes.\n\n## What this does NOT establish\n\n- That memory exhaustion caused the 2026-09-05 wedge; it remains the leading candidate\n  (`retiring-coronary-muntjac`). This bounds it either way.\n- Whether mcp's 4.64 GB peak is steady-state growth or allocator retention.\n- The actual concurrent gate count in practice versus the permitted 4.\n- Whether 15% holds beyond the measured window — which IS the metric's entire published\n  history (48 hourly buckets from 2026-09-03T17:00Z); the margin is one point.\n- Whether the metric-math dead-man behaves as intended in CloudWatch: syntactically validated,\n  not applied here, so runtime behaviour is unproven.\n- Whether these limits hold under a workload outside the measured window."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788655870439334001,"uuid":"5e985c68-f80a-4995-b4e4-6ae52aa6cbd4"}